  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 257 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nAn unseasonably strong mid-level ridge with 500 hPa heights up \nto 600 dam over Texas continues to expand ENE toward the mid- \nMississippi River and Ohio River valleys this afternoon. Initial\nascent on the periphery of the ridge has maintained a general \nNNW to SSE axis of high-based showers drifting across the \nforecast area this afternoon. A diminishing reservoir of MUCAPE \nwith eastward extent across the north half of Illinois has \nallowed some sparse embedded lightning to approach the far west \nand southwest CWA. Overall expectations are for isolated to \nscattered showers to continue tracking across the remainder of \nthe CWA through early evening. Clearing has lead to surface-\nbased destabilization behind an effective surface front over \ncentral Illinois. While stronger convection has brushed the \nextreme southern CWA, remaining activity is expected to remain \nsouth this afternoon.\n\nAppreciable deep layer ascent driven by a mid/upper-level jet \ncore passing north of the area tonight and an approaching \ncompact mid-level wave currently over southwest Nebraska will \ninteract with an expansive MUCAPE reservoir advecting \nnortheastward toward the area tonight. Convection will likely \nquickly develop over far northern Iowa late this evening and \nexpand eastward to southern Wisconsin in response to a \nstrengthening LLJ focused toward far southwest Wisconsin \novernight. With the veering LLJ becoming nearly perpendicular to\nthe low-level front, significantly high PWAT values at or above\n2 inches (nearly 250 percent of normal for the second half of \nSeptember), and relatively slow storm motions, there is growing \nconcern that an axis of significant rainfall develops just north\nof the IL/WI state line overnight. This activity would then \nslowly translate southward over northern Illinois toward \ndaybreak and through at least mid-morning. Rainfall rates of 2 \ninches per hour or higher (similar to what was observed early \nThursday morning), could lead to flash flooding, especially for \nurbanized areas around Rockford and the Chicago metro. A Flood \nWatch for flash flooding has been issued for a vast majority of \nthe Chicago metro and areas around and north of I-88 late \ntonight through Saturday morning, with the greatest concern in \nthe 5-10am window. Severe thunderstorms are not expected as this\nactivity will remain elevated, but localized sub-severe gusts \ncannot be ruled out.\n\nConvection may fester over some of the area late morning \nthrough much of the afternoon, but coverage is expected to \nremain low. The low-level environment will quickly destabilize \nto the southwest Saturday afternoon, with the associated airmass\nbeginning to advect northeastward toward the forecast area. \nAnother mid-level wave currently over southeast Colorado is \nprogged to cross southern Wisconsin very late in the afternoon \nand evening, aligning with a slow-moving surface low and \ninitiating renewed convection over or just north of the state \nline by early evening. Organized strong to marginally severe \nconvection cannot be ruled out in a narrow corridor west of the \nI-39 corridor where low-level destabilization, delayed low-level\nwarming/capping, and modest low-level shear support a very \nconditional localized damaging wind and brief tornado threat \nlate afternoon into early evening.\n\nThe flooding risk will increase again with the evening \nconvection as most ingredients for flooding present in the \nmorning will remain in place during the evening. Convection may \nthen stall somewhere over the central or southern CWA overnight \ninto Sunday morning as the low-level jet strengthens and veers,\nleading to the potential for a local corridor of significant \nrainfall totals and additional flooding. A new Flood Watch will \nlikely be required for much of the night for a large portion of \nthe forecast area.\n\nWhile strong high pressure building across Ontario and Quebec \nthrough early next week should provide a feed of fairly dry air \ntoward the area, another compact mid-level low traversing the \neffective cold front may result in additional heavy rain across \ncentral Illinois, including some of the forecast area, on \nMonday. Dry and cool conditions will then become more favored \nbeginning Tuesday and especially later in the week.\n\nKluber",
